>It means that your driver code tree isn't sync'ed with the latest=20
>tree. Just rebase your tree to the latest one. Or...=20
The trick is, I have a kernel tree that only contains this Kconfig. So thes=
e two should be the same, because I copied them.
No matter, I edited the patch manually and hope it will perform as it shoul=
d.

>I found that the recent kernel has linux/ihex.h helper functions.=20
>They might help for simplifying the code. But, this can be done later=20
>as a a clean-up.=20
Yes, I also discovered this helper function, but it supports only some kind=
 of binary ihex format. It did not accept
the text .ihx files.

>=20
>> diff -Nur a/sound/usb/6fire/pcm.c b/sound/usb/6fire/pcm.c=20
>> --- a/sound/usb/6fire/pcm.c=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A01970-01-01 01:00:00.0=
00000000 +0100=20
>> +++ b/sound/usb/6fire/pcm.c=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A0=C2=A02011-01-20 23:07:24.0=
00000000 +0100=20
>...=20
>> +/* keep next two synced with=20
>> + * FW_EP_W_MAX_PACKET_SIZE[] and RATES_MAX_PACKET_SIZE */=20
>> +static const int RATES_IN_PACKET_SIZE[] =3D { 228, 228, 420, 420, 404, =
404 };=20
>> +static const int RATES_OUT_PACKET_SIZE[] =3D { 228, 228, 420, 420, 604,=
 604 };=20
>> +static const int RATES[] =3D { 44100, 48000, 88200, 96000, 176400, 1920=
00 };=20
>=20
>Any reason to use capital letters for these?=20
>Because they are const?=20
That was exactly the idea.

>Why x86-dependent?=20
I thought so because of the firmware stuff. If bit- or byte-order are chang=
ed, the firmware uploading might not work. If I figured out the #ifdef stuf=
f for bit and byte order, I will remove this dependency.

Thanks,
Torsten
